course: everyday
lesson: 98
title: Friendship troubles, being left out and bullying
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Real friends are kind to you all the time, not only when nobody else is around; most friendships survive arguments if both cool down, talk and say sorry, and you do not have to stay close to someone who treats you badly.
explain: Everyone deserves someone to play with: if children say you cannot play, or you are always picked last, an adult on duty or a teacher can help, for example by choosing teams in a fairer way.
explain: Bullying is when someone hurts, threatens or teases you again and again on purpose; it is never your fault, and the way to stop it is to tell a parent and teacher and keep telling until it stops, even if the bully says not to.
explain: Online, do not reply to mean messages; save them as evidence and tell an adult; and never join in being mean to fit in, because you can belong to a group without hurting anyone.
explain: A girl in Brisbane gets nasty messages in a game chat; she does not answer, takes screenshots, shows her dad, and the next day the school and the game's moderators (the people who keep the chat safe) stop it.
fact: Most friendships survive arguments if both people cool down, talk and say sorry.
fact: Real friends are kind all the time, not only when no one else is around.
fact: Talking about a party in front of people who were not invited can make them feel left out.
fact: You do not have to stay close to someone who is unkind; you can be polite but spend less time with them.
fact: Letting a friend copy your homework does not help them learn; explaining it does.
fact: If children say you cannot play, ask an adult on duty or find another group.
fact: Teachers can choose teams in fair ways so nobody is always picked last.
fact: Bullying is never the fault of the person being bullied.
fact: Bullies rely on silence, so telling a trusted adult is the way to stop it.
fact: Online, do not reply to mean messages; save them and tell an adult.
fact: You can be part of a group without hurting others.
fact: Teasing about someone's looks, skin colour, religion or family is not allowed, and adults must stop it.
fact: When a parent has a serious illness, keeping up your normal activities and talking to a trusted adult helps you and your parent.
exercise: Kai's mum has a serious illness. What helps him? | Keep doing his normal things and talk to a trusted adult | Stop seeing his friends so he can stay at home and worry about her | Keep his feelings secret so nobody is upset | Stay home from school every day to watch her
exercise: You fall out with your best friend. What is the best way to fix it? | Cool down, then talk and both say sorry if needed | Never speak to them again | Get other friends to take your side | Wait for them to apologise first, however long it takes, even for weeks
exercise: A friend plays with you only when nobody else is around. What should you do? | Play with others who treat you well too | Keep waiting for them to be free | Do whatever they ask so they like you more | Follow them around when they are with others
exercise: Why should you keep party talk quiet at school? | It can make children who were not invited feel left out | Parties are against school rules | Teachers do not like hearing about parties | Others might hear about it and turn up at the party without being asked
exercise: A friend is often unkind to you and you want to stop being close. What is fair? | Be polite but spend less time with them | Tell everyone the bad things they did | Be unkind back so they know how it feels | Stay best friends so they do not get upset
exercise: Your friend asks to copy your homework. What should you do? | Say no kindly and offer to explain it | Let them copy, because friends share | Give them the wrong answers as a joke | Tell the whole class that they asked
exercise: Some children say you cannot play with them. What can you do? | Ask the adult on duty for help or find another group | Stand right next to them and keep asking until they give in and let you | Tell them they are horrible and walk off | Sit alone and never ask anyone again
exercise: Ben is always picked last for teams in PE. What can he do? | Tell the teacher how it feels so they choose teams another way | Pretend to be ill whenever there is PE | Pick on the child who chose last | Practise on his own every evening and never mention it to anyone at all
exercise: Someone keeps calling you names and pushing you. What should you do? | Tell a parent and teacher, and keep telling until it stops | Wait for it to stop by itself | Push them back much harder | Change the way you look or talk so that they stop picking on you
exercise: A bully says, «If you tell, it will be worse.» What should you do? | Tell a trusted adult anyway | Keep quiet so it does not get worse | Do what the bully wants from now on | Only tell your best friend
exercise: Why do bullies tell children to keep quiet? | Bullies rely on silence to keep going | Telling really does always make it worse | Teachers are not allowed to help | It is a fair rule between children
exercise: Someone sends you mean messages online. What should you do? | Not reply, save the messages and tell an adult | Reply with something even meaner | Delete everything so nobody sees it | Post the messages for everyone to see so they know how mean the person is
exercise: Your group is laughing at a quiet boy and you are tempted to join in to fit in. What is right? | Do not join in | Laugh a little so the group likes you | Join in, but only with small jokes | Laugh, then say sorry to him later
exercise: A girl is teased about the colour of her skin. What is true? | This teasing is not allowed, and an adult must be told | It is only a joke, so she should just ignore it and laugh along with them | She should change her friends to stop it | It is fine if everyone is laughing
test: Two best friends in Austin argue about a game and both feel bad the next day. What usually works? | They talk calmly and both say sorry | They each find a new best friend | They wait for the teacher to sort it out | They pretend the argument never happened
test: Which friend is a real friend? | One who is kind to you whether or not others are there | One who only sits with you when the cool group is busy | One who laughs at you with other children | One who makes you do things to stay friends
test: Why is explaining homework better than letting a friend copy it? | Explaining helps your friend really learn it | Copying is slower than explaining | Explaining makes you look cleverer | Teachers always give rewards to children who explain things
test: A boy in Cork is pushed and threatened every day on the way home. He thinks telling is snitching. What is true? | Telling an adult about bullying is right, not snitching | Telling makes him a snitch, so he should keep quiet about it | He should deal with it alone to be brave | It will stop by itself if he ignores it
test: A child told a teacher once about bullying, but it is still going on. What next? | Tell again, and tell a parent too, until it stops | Give up, because telling does not work | Fight the bully, since telling failed | Change schools without telling anyone the real reason why
test: Why should you save mean online messages instead of deleting them? | They are evidence that helps adults stop it | So you can read them again later | So you can send them back to the bully | Because deleting messages is against the law
test: Your group starts leaving a girl out on purpose. Which shows real strength? | Not joining in and being kind to her | Going along with the group to stay in | Staying silent so nobody notices you | Leaving the group and telling nobody why
test: Some children in Montreal keep laughing at a boy because his family is different. What is the right thing to do? | Tell an adult, because this teasing is not allowed | Laugh too, since he is used to it | Tell him to keep his family a secret | Stay out of it, because what happens to him is not your business
test: Ella talks about her sleepover in front of two girls who were not invited. They look hurt. What could she have done? | Talked about it later, away from them | Invited the whole class to stop it | Told them they were not invited on purpose | Talked about it more loudly to make it fun
test: they said i cant play their game at break | Ask the adult on duty or find another group | Push in and play anyway | Go and cry where no one sees | Tell them you will never speak to them ever again
test: can i copy ur homework pls, didnt get it | Better to explain it so they learn | Yes, friends always share answers | Yes, if you change a few words | Yes, if the teacher will not notice
test: me n amy had a big fight, friendship's over | Most friendships survive if you cool down, talk and say sorry | Yes, one big fight is enough to end a friendship for good | Find a new best friend tomorrow | Never speak to her again
test: shes nice to me only when nobodys around | Real friends are kind all the time | That is what real friends do | She is a true friend, just in secret | Be the same way with her
explain: A boy in Ohio was bullied for months and told no one; he began to fake illness to stay home, until his teacher found out and the bullying was stopped.
explain: A group of girls in Leeds shared a mean photo of a classmate in a group chat; it spread to the whole school, the girl was very upset, and the girls who shared it were suspended, which means sent home from school for some days.
fact: Bullying that is kept secret usually goes on, while telling a trusted adult helps to stop it.
fact: In British slang, to «fancy» someone means to like them as a boyfriend or girlfriend.
fact: «A new fella» is slang for a new boyfriend or man friend.
fact: Sharing a mean message or photo hurts the person each time it is passed on, and people who share it can get in serious trouble.
test: someone sent a mean pic of a kid in our group chat, everyone's laughing | Don't share it, tell a trusted adult | Share it, everyone else did it already | Add a funny comment | Send it to more chats
test: getting bullied every day, gonna pretend i'm sick to stay home | Tell a trusted adult so they can stop it | Stay home for the rest of the year, it's easier | Bully someone smaller | Keep it secret forever
test: they leave me out on purpose every lunch, is that bullying | It can be, tell a trusted adult | No, that's just normal | Only hitting counts as bullying | You should leave them out too
test: omg did u see, someone posted a pic of Ellie asleep on the bus with her mouth open, the whole year's got it now, it's sooo funny. what's the real lesson | Sharing a mean photo hurts her each time, and sharers get in trouble | It is only a joke, so sharing it does no harm at all to anyone in the class | It is fine to share as long as you did not take it | The photo is harmless once everyone has seen it
test: Liam reckons the new kid got bullied so bad at his last school he had to go to hospital, his brother told him. what's probably the case | It is probably a rumour that got bigger each time it was told | It must be true, because a brother said it | It is true, since bullied children always end up in hospital | It is true if the new kid seems really quiet and shy in class
test: heard Noah told on the boys who were picking on him n now everyone's calling him a snitch, gonna tell the group chat. what should u do | Do not spread it; telling an adult about bullying was right | Spread it, because everyone should know who snitched on the older boys | Spread it, but leave Noah's name out of it | Spread it only to the boys who were picking on him
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises bullying? | Hurting, threatening or teasing repeated on purpose | A single disagreement between two equal friends | Any occasion upon which a child is not chosen first | Teasing which the victim has in some way provoked
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the reporting of bullying to a trusted adult is advised. | Bullying relies upon silence and tends to persist when concealed | Reporting ensures that the pupil reporting is moved to another class | Adults are thereby obliged to punish the entire class equally | Reporting obliges the bully to apologise in public at once
exam: Pursuant to the receipt of offensive messages by a pupil in an online forum, ascertain the appropriate course of action. | Refrain from replying, retain the messages and inform an adult | Reply in equally offensive terms so as to deter the sender | Delete every message immediately and inform nobody at all | Forward the messages to classmates in order to obtain their support
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning responsibility for bullying. | It is never the fault of the person who is bullied | It is partly the fault of a victim who appears different | It is the fault of the victim if he does not fight back | It is the fault of whoever first reports it to an adult
test: my best mate ditched me "for her boyfriend" n now i'm on my own at break | She is spending time with a boyfriend; it hurts, so tell her kindly how you feel and play with others too | It means she hates you now, so start spreading rumours about her at school | It means boyfriends are not allowed at your age, so tell a teacher on her | Tease her about the boyfriend
test: they left Ella out cos "her mum's got a new fella" | Her mum has a new partner; that is no reason to leave Ella out, so ask her to play | It is a fair reason, so you should leave her out too until things settle down | It means Ella is in trouble | Ask Ella lots about the new man
test: Jake goes "u fancy Ben, admit it" every lunch n i only like him as a mate | Jake is teasing that you want Ben as a boyfriend; say no, you are friends, and tell an adult if he keeps on | You must stop being Ben's friend straight away so that the teasing stops for good | You have to admit it even if it is not true, because then he will stop asking | Tease Jake back about someone else
